cally changed as computing  power and so I  did a quick google search and I saw that  as of 2007 there's the Concord solver  that can use these techniques as well as  all kinds of fancy cutting planes and  other things to solve traveling salesman  instances optimally with up to 25,000  cities so I hope that that you know  impresses you and and that you see how  your contributions are still informing  us today and the reason I was so  interested in this is at the time I was  working on approximation algorithms to  the graphic TSP problem so that problem  is the Traveling Salesman problem on  shortest path metrics of undirected  unweighted graphs and I was specifically  interested in approximating solutions to  this problem in cubic bipartite graphs  so this these are graphs of degree 3 and  bipartite and so this this class of  graphs allows us to leverage the  structure to get improved approximation  factors but also that keeps much of the  complexity of the original problem and  it also is interesting because it ties  into Barnett's conjecture a conjecture  in structural graph theory that says  that if it's true every bipartite cubic  polyhedron is Hamiltonian and so i'll  explain in a second after showing the  result why why this relates to that so  the the main result is that given a  cubic bipartite graph there's a  polynomial time algorithm that computes  a solution with at most 97 and minus 2  edges and this is joint work with my  advisor are Ravi and so the way we we  obtain this result is we want to find a  cycle cover in the graph with relatively  few cycles so the the general approach  if you're able to do that is you find a  cycle cover with K cycles in your graph  you compress each cycle into a single  node and compute a spanning tree in this  this new grip compressed graph and then  you take your cycle cover and and join  it with two copies of the spanning tree  to get a solution to the Traveling  Salesman problem with n plus 2 K minus 1  edges and so ou