ormulated this  model as the orders arrive you need to  make an immediate decision whether the  retailer accepts or rejects the order  but you may have time before you have to  decide how the order is allocated and so  11 Avenue that I'm pursuing right now is  to see if a better than one-half  competitive algorithm can be formulated  by allowing recourse in this sense then  I'm going to quickly just mention some  of the other topics within this area of  omni-channel optimization that I find  very interesting and I'm currently  pursuing results on so one of them is is  trying to formulate stochastic  optimization problems so instead of this  online setting where you have an  adversary feeding you the orders you can  say that you know  certain prior demand probability  distributions over the demands and try  and solve various sort of news vendor  like optimization problems in this  setting and we've had some I've had some  success of my with my advisors on  solving these formulations but this is  still work in progress and then a nice  extension of this that translates back a  little bit into the the theory community  is is understanding learning rates from  observed data so in reality in it's  unlikely to be known what these  probability distributions of demand for  items are as well as the probability  distributions for order failures and so  one interesting set of problems is where  you have observed data of historical  demands for items and the successor and  failure of of these orders that were  accepted and using that to learn these  rates on the fly and make optimal or  near optimal decisions and there's been  some work in the area of sample average  approximation for trying to use this  historical demand and prove guarantees  of if you want a solution that is within  a certain factor of epsilon close to  optimal with a certain probability upper  bound on the number of samples needed  and but I have some preliminary results  that that I'm developing in this are